.TH CTYPE 3
.SH NAME
isalpha, isupper, islower, isdigit, isxdigit, isalnum, isspace, ispunct, isprint, isgraph, iscntrl, isascii, toupper, tolower, toascii \- character classification and conversion
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ctype.h>

isalpha(c)
\&...

toupper(c)
tolower(c)
toascii(c)
int c;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
These macros classify characters by looking them up in a table. Each
is nonzero if
.I c
is in its class, and 0 if it is not:
.TP 10
.B isalpha
a letter
.TP
.B isupper
an upper case letter
.TP
.B islower
a lower case letter
.TP
.B isdigit
a digit, 0 to 9
.TP
.B isxdigit
a hexadecimal digit: 0 to 9, A to F, or a to f
.TP
.B isalnum
a letter or a digit
.TP
.B isspace
a space, tab, newline, carriage return, vertical tab or form feed
.TP
.B ispunct
a punctuation character: a printing character that is not a letter, a
digit or a space
.TP
.B isprint
a printing character, from 040 (space) to 0176 (~)
.TP
.B isgraph
a printing character other than the space
.TP
.B iscntrl
a control character: below 040, or 0177 (delete)
.TP
.B isascii
an ASCII character, from 0 to 0177
.PP
They are defined for every value of
.I c
from \-1 (EOF) to 255, and for no others; the characters from 0200 to
0377 are in none of the classes.
.PP
.B toupper
converts a lower case letter to upper case, and
.B tolower
an upper case letter to lower case; any other character is returned
unchanged. These two are functions.
.B _toupper
and
.B _tolower
are macros that do the same faster, but only for a
.I c
known to be a letter of the other case.
.B toascii
clears all but the lowest 7 bits of
.IR c .
.SH SEE ALSO
string(3), ascii(5)
.SH HISTORY
The classifying macros, and
.B toupper
and
.BR tolower ,
first appeared in Version 7 AT&T UNIX.
.BR isgraph ,
.B _toupper
and
.B _tolower
first appeared in System III, where toupper and tolower took their
present form, leaving characters that are not letters unchanged.
